          'Industrial plastic packaging is classified under HS heading 3923, which covers articles for the conveyance or packing of goods, of plastics. The heading includes a range of product types distinguished by form and function. Subheading 3923.10 covers boxes, cases, crates and similar articles; 3923.21 and 3923.29 cover sacks and bags (including those with a strip of polymer of vinyl chloride); 3923.30 covers carboys, bottles, flasks and similar articles; and 3923.90 covers other articles including pallets and IBCs.',
          'The plastics commonly used for industrial packaging include polyethylene (PE) — both high-density (HDPE) and low-density (LDPE) — polypropylene (PP), polyethylene terephthalate (PET) and, to a lesser extent, polycarbonate (PC) and other engineering plastics. The material is selected based on the required strength, flexibility, chemical resistance, temperature range, transparency and compatibility with the packed goods. HDPE is widely used for rigid containers such as drums, crates and IBCs due to its stiffness, impact resistance and chemical resistance. LDPE and LLDPE are used for flexible films and bags. PP is used for woven sacks, caps and rigid containers.',
          'Key performance characteristics of industrial plastic packaging include mechanical strength (tensile, impact, compression), dimensional stability, wall thickness uniformity, chemical resistance to the packed product, temperature resistance, stackability, reusability and recyclability. For packaging used with food, pharmaceuticals or hazardous goods, regulatory compliance with food-contact, pharmaceutical or dangerous goods transport regulations is essential. Packaging for international transport may need to meet UN performance standards for dangerous goods packaging.',
